<p class="gna-lede">An orbital fracture is a break in one of the thin bones that form the eye socket, most often its floor or inner wall. Many small fractures heal without an operation, but double vision that does not settle, a sunken-looking eye or a trapped eye muscle can mean surgery is needed, usually within the first two weeks. Every suspected orbital fracture needs an eye examination as well as a CT scan.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-orbital-fracture">What is an orbital (eye socket) fracture?</h2>
<p>The orbit is the bony cup that holds your eye, the six small muscles that move it, its nerves and a protective cushion of fat. The rim at the front of the socket is thick and strong. The floor, which is also the roof of the maxillary sinus, and the inner wall beside the nose are as thin as an eggshell.</p>
<p>A <strong>blow-out fracture</strong> happens when something larger than the socket opening, such as a fist, a cricket ball, a handlebar or the road in a two-wheeler fall, strikes the eye area. The sudden rise in pressure pushes the eye backwards and the thin floor or inner wall "blows out" into the sinus, while the rim itself may stay intact. Other orbital fractures involve the rim and are often part of a broken cheekbone or a wider facial injury. In India, road-traffic accidents, assaults, falls at home or work, and sports injuries are the usual causes.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The floor and inner wall of the eye socket are thin and break easily; this is called a blow-out fracture.</li>
    <li>Double vision, numbness of the cheek and a sunken eye are the signs that matter most.</li>
    <li>Do not blow your nose after a significant injury around the eye until a doctor says it is safe.</li>
    <li>Many small fractures settle with observation; when surgery is needed, it is usually planned within about two weeks.</li>
    <li>A child with severe pain on moving the eye, nausea or fainting after an eye injury needs same-day assessment.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="symptoms">What are the symptoms of an orbital fracture?</h2>
<p>Swelling and bruising around the eye ("a black eye") are expected after any blow to the face, so they do not by themselves mean the bone is broken. These are the features that doctors look for:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Double vision (diplopia)</strong>, especially when looking up or down. It can come from swelling, a bruised eye muscle, or a muscle caught in the fracture.</li>
  <li><strong>Pain when moving the eye</strong>, particularly when looking upwards.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness or tingling</strong> of the cheek, the side of the nose, the upper lip or the upper front teeth and gum. The infraorbital nerve runs through the floor of the socket and is often bruised.</li>
  <li><strong>A sunken or lower-set eye</strong> (enophthalmos). This is frequently hidden by swelling at first and becomes noticeable a week or two later as the swelling goes down.</li>
  <li><strong>Puffiness that crackles under the fingers</strong>, or eyelids that swell up suddenly after blowing the nose, a sign that air has escaped from the sinus into the tissues.</li>
  <li><strong>A nosebleed</strong> on the injured side, from blood collecting in the sinus.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The nose-blowing warning</h3>
<p>Because a blow-out fracture creates an opening between the eye socket and the sinus, blowing your nose can force air into the socket. The eyelids may puff up within seconds. Usually this settles by itself, but occasionally the trapped air raises the pressure behind the eye and threatens vision, and it may also carry bacteria from the sinus into the socket. After any significant injury around the eye, avoid blowing your nose, sneeze with your mouth open, and avoid straining or heavy lifting until your surgeon advises otherwise, commonly for two to three weeks. Ask before you fly.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Go to the nearest emergency department or call 108 immediately if:</strong> your vision becomes blurred, dim or dark; the eye is very painful, bulging or feels tense; you cannot open the eyelid; there is a cut on the eyeball itself; or there are signs of a head injury such as repeated vomiting, confusion, drowsiness or clear watery fluid from the nose. Bleeding behind the eye (retrobulbar haemorrhage) is rare, but it can damage sight within hours and needs urgent treatment.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="eye-check">Why an eye check matters as much as the scan</h2>
<p>The broken bone usually heals; it is the eye that most needs protecting. A force strong enough to break the socket can also injure the eye itself, causing bleeding inside the eye, a tear in the retina or damage to the optic nerve. These problems are not always obvious to the patient. For that reason, every patient with a suspected orbital fracture should have their vision, pupils, eye pressure and the back of the eye checked by an eye specialist (ophthalmologist), ideally before any decision about surgery.</p>
<p>The fracture itself is assessed with a <strong>CT scan</strong>, which shows the size of the defect, whether muscle or fat has dropped into the sinus, and whether other facial bones are broken. Plain X-rays miss many orbital fractures. Your eye movements and any double vision may be charted (an orthoptic assessment) and the position of each eye measured, so that changes can be tracked accurately at follow-up visits.</p>
<h3>Children: the "white-eyed" blow-out</h3>
<p>In children and teenagers the bones are springy. The floor of the socket can crack, trap the muscle that moves the eye, and then spring back like a trapdoor. The eye may look surprisingly normal, with very little bruising, yet the child has severe pain on looking up, nausea or vomiting, a slow pulse, or faints. This is caused by a nerve reflex triggered by the trapped muscle, and the muscle can be permanently damaged within hours. It needs same-day specialist assessment and usually surgery within 24 to 48 hours.</p>

<h2 id="does-it-need-surgery">Does an orbital fracture always need surgery?</h2>
<p>No. Many orbital fractures, especially small floor or inner-wall fractures without a trapped muscle, heal well on their own. Double vision caused by swelling usually improves steadily over one to two weeks. The decision depends on how the eye behaves as the swelling settles, which is why a review at about seven to ten days after the injury is common.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Usual approach</th><th>Typical timing</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Small fracture, no trapped muscle, double vision improving</td><td>Observation, no nose-blowing, follow-up visits</td><td>Review at 1–2 weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trapped muscle with nausea, slow pulse or severe pain (more common in children)</td><td>Urgent surgery to free the muscle</td><td>Within 24–48 hours</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Double vision that is not improving, with tissue caught in the fracture on CT</td><td>Surgery to release the tissue and repair the floor</td><td>Usually within about 2 weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Large defect likely to make the eye sink, or a sunken eye already visible</td><td>Reconstruction of the socket</td><td>Usually within about 2 weeks</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sunken eye or double vision noticed months later</td><td>Secondary (late) reconstruction</td><td>Planned; more complex</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Waiting more than a few weeks is not ideal when surgery is clearly needed, because scar tissue forms and the displaced fat and muscle become harder to restore. Late correction is still possible, but its results are less predictable. Equally, operating on a fracture that would have healed well by itself adds risk without benefit, so the timing is decided case by case, with you, after the eye examination and CT scan.</p>

