<h2 id="surgery">What happens during orbital fracture surgery?</h2>
<p>Surgery is done under general anaesthesia in hospital and usually involves a short stay. The surgeon reaches the floor of the socket through a small cut on the inside of the lower eyelid (the transconjunctival approach), through a fine incision just below the lashes, or through an existing wound. The trapped muscle and fat are gently lifted back into the socket, and the gap in the bone is covered with an implant, commonly a thin titanium mesh or plate shaped to the socket, a sheet of material that dissolves over time, or occasionally the patient's own bone. Fractures of the inner wall may be reached through a small incision near the inner corner of the eye.</p>
<p>Planning is based on the CT scan. For complex fractures, 3D planning can help shape the implant to mirror the uninjured side; read more about <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">how 3D virtual planning is used in facial surgery</a>. Orbital fractures often come with other facial fractures, particularly a <a href="/blogs/cheekbone-fracture-zygomatic-signs-surgery-recovery/">broken cheekbone (zygomatic fracture)</a>, which can usually be repaired during the same operation. Your vision is checked closely in the hours after surgery.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-risks">Recovery and risks</h2>
<h3>What recovery usually looks like</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>First 48–72 hours:</strong> swelling and bruising are at their worst. Keep your head raised on two or three pillows and use cold compresses wrapped in a clean cloth, as advised.</li>
  <li><strong>First two to three weeks:</strong> no nose-blowing, heavy lifting or straining; sneeze with your mouth open. Antibiotics or decongestant sprays may be prescribed. Practice varies, and the evidence for routine antibiotics is mixed.</li>
  <li><strong>Returning to work:</strong> often one to two weeks for desk work, and longer for physical jobs or if you have other injuries.</li>
  <li><strong>Double vision:</strong> if present, it often improves over weeks; some patients take several months, and eye exercises guided by an orthoptist may help.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness:</strong> cheek and lip numbness usually improves over several months, but in some people a patch of altered feeling remains.</li>
  <li><strong>Sport:</strong> avoid contact sport for at least six weeks, or for as long as your surgeon advises.</li>
</ul>
<p>Recovery varies with the size of the fracture, any other injuries and your general health, so your own timeline may be quicker or slower than this.</p>
<h3>Risks to discuss with your surgeon</h3>
<p>Like any operation, orbital surgery carries risks, which should be weighed against the risks of not operating:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Double vision that persists or, less commonly, appears after surgery</li>
  <li>An eye that remains slightly sunken, or sits slightly higher than the other</li>
  <li>Lower eyelid changes: the lid may pull down or turn inwards or outwards, depending on the incision used</li>
  <li>Persistent numbness of the cheek or upper lip</li>
  <li>Infection, or movement of the implant, occasionally requiring further surgery</li>
  <li>Bleeding behind the eye and loss of vision, which are rare but are the most serious risks</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/blogs/side-effects-of-maxillofacial-surgery-risks-complications-and-prevention/">the side effects of maxillofacial surgery</a> explains general surgical risks and how they are reduced.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>See a maxillofacial surgeon and an eye specialist promptly, on the same day or the next, if after a blow to the face you notice double vision, pain on moving the eye, numbness of the cheek or upper lip, a sunken eye, or one eye sitting lower than the other. If you are unsure whether anything is broken, it is safer to be examined than to wait. Simple steps for the first hour are covered in our <a href="/blogs/facial-trauma-first-aid-steps-everyone-must-know-in-emergencies/">facial trauma first aid guide</a>.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/facial-injuries">facial injury treatment</a> team in Gachibowli, Hyderabad assesses patients and, when surgery is needed, operates at NABH-accredited partner hospitals (CARE Hospitals, KIMS, Apollo Spectra and Yashoda). The 24/7 facial-trauma line is <strong>+91 90006 66476</strong>. For any emergency, such as loss of vision, heavy bleeding or a head injury, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department first.</p>
<p>Cashless insurance may be available for trauma surgery, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation. If the injury happened in a road accident, keep copies of the hospital records and CT report, as insurers usually ask for them.</p>
